          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            behave like
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+objects"} path space objects
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fibers"} homotopy fibers
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
          %p
            On the other hand, if type theory contains a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Type"} Type
            %em points
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Paths
                      %mi Type
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Paths_{Type}(X,Y)
            %em univalence axiom
            says that these two notions of “sameness” for types are the same.
          %p
            Extensionality principles like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+extensionality"} propositional extensionality
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+of+indiscernibles"} identity of indiscernibles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consistency"} consistency
            of univalence means that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            without univalence, one cannot define any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            that provably distinguishes isomorphic
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
          %p
            The name
            %em univalence
            (due to Voevodsky) comes from the following reasoning. A fibration or bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon E\to B
            of some sort is commonly said to be
            %em universal
            if every other bundle of the same sort is a pullback of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in a unique way (up to homotopy). Less commonly, a bundle is said to be
            %em versal
            if every other bundle is a pullback of it in
            %em some
            way, not necessarily unique. By contrast, a bundle is said to be
            %em univalent
            if every other bundle is a pullback of it in
            %em at most one
            way (up to homotopy). In the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            theory, a univalent bundle is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
          %p
            The univalence axiom does not
            %em literally
            say that anything is univalent in this sense. However, it is
            %em equivalent
            to saying that the canonical fibration over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            is univalent: every fibration with
            %em small
            fibers is an essentially unique pullback of this one (while those with large fibers are not, they are pullbacks of the next higher
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Type
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type_1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+Book"} HoTT Book
            (syntactically) and
            %a{:href => "#GepnerKock12"} Gepner-Kock
            (semantically).
          %p
            Univalence is a commonly assumed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Voevodsky"} Voevodsky
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+for+mathematics"} univalent foundations for mathematics
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We work in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
          %p
            There are multiple notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+types"} equivalence types
            in
            = succeed "," do
